<p>Italian Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni said Tuesday that AI-generated images falsely depicting her were circulating online, including one showing a woman with her face sitting on a bed in underwear, and that some political opponents had presented the images as real. Meloni reposted one altered image on Facebook to identify it as fake, alongside a screenshot of a user identified as &quot;Roberto&quot; who had reshared it while saying she should be ashamed. Meloni said deepfakes can deceive, manipulate and target people, and urged users to verify content before believing it and to think before sharing it. In a separate proceeding in Sardinia, two Italians, a father and son, are accused of publishing manipulated pornographic videos in 2020 using Meloni&#x27;s face; Meloni testified by video link in 2024, and the trial is ongoing.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/1ZZ7mJRcyU-EKSTiY7GwOW5s2p0y0zuCHMgLbYl-QFknQ2QebpGoM2Qm_k6mVmtAO9cVMldn55j-pOSexK6CgJVaylT4W2M2Md8NVYVWL6p1S3dvGQZ94mHG8nAwzUlXE5ntIVzW_DlNDqweTLAb7n0LtudxrTnmEusHw6LvOw' alt='Italian Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Public reaction: The fake image drew criticism of Meloni from users who believed it was authentic before she identified it as AI-generated.</li><li>Image details: Quotidiano.net described the face as Meloni&#x27;s but the body as an AI invention, with the image processed to remove ordinary imperfections.</li><li>Political response: ANSA reported that women parliamentarians expressed solidarity with Meloni after she denounced the fake images.</li><li>Broader misuse: Avvenire said manipulated images of political figures can spread fake news, discredit targets or fuel sexualized abuse.</li></ul><blockquote>The issue, however, goes beyond me. Deepfakes are a dangerous tool because they can deceive, manipulate, and target anyone. I can defend myself. Many others cannot - Giorgia Meloni</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Giorgia Meloni: Meloni said the cr